WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
Mexico's request for starbase airspace talks could constrain how fast SpaceX scales to hundreds

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:11.880
of annual launches. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 176. Stoke Space's booster is at the

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:17.300
cape, falcon 9 flies this weekend, and Belgium is opening a satellite factory, then the

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.880
counterpoint and the engineering deep dive. President Claudia Scheinbaum said, as tech times

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:28.100
reports, that Mexico has asked the US Embassy to meet on how Mexican airspace is used

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:34.200
for SpaceX launches, on caps on launch cadence, and on relocating starbase if that is required,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.540
a day after she called for meetings following debris on Tamalipa's beaches.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.540
Mexico's air navigation service recorded the starship upper stage over Mexico for seven minutes

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:51.100
on November 14. After a federal aviation administration, or FAA, notice the day before.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:58.260
4. The FAA is weighing as many as 456 star-based launches a year, more than 18 times today's

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:04.300
25 launch cap. Those flights already send notices to air missions or no-tams that shut

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:10.180
Mexican airspace, and a starship flight on July 24 forced a Guatemalan Medevac jet to

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:15.520
reverse near the Belize-Mexico border. Hazard area notices have kept marking danger

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.980
from the ground to unlimited altitude, and airlines have to coordinate whenever routes

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.140
are expected to enter Mexican territory.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:28.040
Shine Bomb called SpaceX a private company whose work affects national airspace and said

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.820
the counterpart is the U.S. government.

00:01:30.949 --> 00:01:36.969
Stoke Space's Nova Pathfinder first stage, NASA Space Flight Reports, finished a 17-day

00:01:36.969 --> 00:01:41.829
road trip from Washington and rolled into the horizontal integration facility at Cape

00:01:41.829 --> 00:01:45.209
Canaveral Space Launch Complex 14 on November 22.

00:01:45.209 --> 00:01:50.369
second. Wet dress rehearsals, loading propellant without lighting the engines, will wake

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:55.749
that rebuilt pad for the first time since the 1960s. The stage then goes back on for

00:01:55.749 --> 00:02:01.349
Zenith engine installation and a static fire, a hold down firing. An upper stage static

00:02:01.349 --> 00:02:06.509
fire is set for December at the Moses Lake test site once the Andromeda engine is attached.

00:02:07.649 --> 00:02:12.629
Protocualification at Moses Lake already cleared 46 structural test objectives and pushed

00:02:12.629 --> 00:02:18.209
the vehicle to 115% of expected flight loads, with hundreds of strain gauges measuring

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:19.429
how the metal flexes.

00:02:20.009 --> 00:02:24.329
Material coupon tests had already measured strength before and after each manufacturing

00:02:24.329 --> 00:02:26.029
step to back that campaign.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:31.630
Northrop Grumman confirmed the Cygnus XL spacecraft is encapsulated and ready.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:37.550
Tech Times reports for a Sunday Falcon 9 flight on Northrop's final 2026 commercial

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:41.410
resupply run to the International Space Station, or ISS.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:48.570
The Antares 330 rocket still has no date, so Northrop is booking this extra Falcon 9 ride.

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:55.170
Vandenberg Space Force Base, KSBY reports, is set for a Saturday evening Falcon 9 carrying

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.350
classified satellites.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.170
Those US Space Force payloads fly from Pad 4E.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:06.350
Europe opened a large satellite factory in Belgium, ZME Science reports, to produce satellites

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:09.370
for a European alternative to Starlink and Starsheald.

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:14.510
Elon Musk posted that Google has expressed interest in renting large blocks of compute

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.690
from SpaceX, a claim Google has not confirmed.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:24.130
Meanwhile, the Federal Communications Commission, or FCC, cleared SpaceX's star-mind 1 million

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:30.690
satellite orbital data center system, Cryptopolitan reports, with rollout gates through 2040 and

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.810
debris mitigation conditions still pending.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:38.850
Environmental groups, or RISSA post-notes, are objecting over orbital debris and light pollution

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:44.357
after that order. Worth keeping an eye on, astronomers and rival operators argue,

00:03:44.957 --> 00:03:50.277
as Breitbart reports that the plan's star-mind constellation will increase radio-frequency interference

00:03:50.277 --> 00:03:57.057
that degrades both optical and radio astronomy. Amazon, Viassat, and the American Astronomical Society

00:03:57.057 --> 00:04:03.137
filed objections the FCC largely set aside. SpaceX must file a debris mitigation plan

00:04:03.137 --> 00:04:05.817
before deploying satellites above 600 kilometers.

00:04:06.377 --> 00:04:12.077
The plan covering operations above 370 miles is still open, and the request for alternative

00:04:12.077 --> 00:04:13.837
disposal methods was deferred.

00:04:14.516 --> 00:04:19.996
The super heavy booster catch, from an engineering standpoint, is a decision to stop flying landing

00:04:19.996 --> 00:04:24.856
legs that would have to take residual propellant and the structural loads of a full ascent and

00:04:24.856 --> 00:04:25.496
a full return.

00:04:26.196 --> 00:04:28.936
Those legs would add hundreds of kilograms of dry mass.

00:04:28.936 --> 00:04:34.096
the vehicle's unfueled weight, taken straight out of payload on every flight and

00:04:34.096 --> 00:04:38.596
they would have to be built, fitted, and qualified even though grid fins folding

00:04:38.596 --> 00:04:43.316
steering surfaces already handled descent control. Separate landing pads would

00:04:43.316 --> 00:04:47.696
have to be designed and qualified too. After the super heavy booster separates

00:04:47.696 --> 00:04:52.396
and flips, grid fins steered during a three-minute engine off coast and point the

00:04:52.396 --> 00:04:57.036
vehicle at the tower without spending propellant on attitude. Then 13 of the

00:04:57.036 --> 00:05:02.296
33 Raptors, the booster's engines, relight for the braking burn, and three stay running

00:05:02.296 --> 00:05:04.036
to hold position beside the tower.

00:05:04.636 --> 00:05:09.236
Throttle those three until thrust matches weight, and the grid fins handle fine attitude.

00:05:09.836 --> 00:05:12.416
That pairing has shown up across multiple flight tests.

00:05:13.076 --> 00:05:17.396
Early flight data showed the catch points below the fins can take the load once the vehicle

00:05:17.396 --> 00:05:17.936
is hovering.

00:05:18.536 --> 00:05:22.196
Instructural testing on the flight article confirmed those points and the surrounding

00:05:22.196 --> 00:05:27.216
structure handle expected loads without extra reinforcement that would put mass back on.

00:05:27.876 --> 00:05:32.836
The tower arms close under the catch points once the booster is stable, then lower it onto the

00:05:32.836 --> 00:05:37.216
launch mount for inspection and refueling. Because the booster is already at the mount,

00:05:37.536 --> 00:05:42.856
the cycle drops from a multi-hour haul and restack off a separate pad to a direct lower and

00:05:42.856 --> 00:05:48.076
refuel sequence that targets under one hour, which does not work if the vehicle has to come from

00:05:48.076 --> 00:05:53.356
another landing site. The same arms can stack the ship upper stage so the pad does not need

00:05:53.356 --> 00:05:58.496
a second stacking system. The sequence trades extra control work in the last seconds of flight

00:05:58.496 --> 00:06:02.896
for fewer parts, less dry mass, and a ground cycle that never leaves the mount.

